\description{
Transforms each variable toward central normality with the Yeo-Johnson
transformation, whose parameter \eqn{\lambda} is fitted robustly
(Raymaekers and Rousseeuw, 2021), so that outlying observations do not
drive it. Unlike the Box-Cox transformation (\code{\link[=robust_boxcox]{robust_boxcox()}}), it
accepts zero and negative values. \code{robust = FALSE} gives the classical
fit, by maximum likelihood, for comparison.
}
\details{
The Yeo-Johnson transformation (Yeo and Johnson, 2000) of a value
\eqn{x \ge 0} is
\deqn{\psi_\lambda(x) = \frac{(1 + x)^\lambda - 1}{\lambda}}
(\eqn{\log(1 + x)} for \eqn{\lambda = 0}), and of a value \eqn{x < 0}
\deqn{\psi_\lambda(x) = -\frac{(1 - x)^{2 - \lambda} - 1}{2 - \lambda}}
(\eqn{-\log(1 - x)} for \eqn{\lambda = 2}). \eqn{\lambda = 1} leaves the
values unchanged; a \eqn{\lambda} below 1 compresses the upper tail, for
right-skewed variables, and a \eqn{\lambda} above 1 compresses the lower
tail, for left-skewed ones.

Each variable is first centered by its median and divided by its MAD, so
that \eqn{\lambda} does not depend on its location or units. It describes
the shape of the distribution on this robust standard scale, and cannot
be compared with the \eqn{\lambda} of \code{\link[=robust_boxcox]{robust_boxcox()}}: on the same data,
it is often much closer to 1.

It suits the variables with zero or negative values, such as
baseline-corrected, derivative or SNV-scaled spectra. Strictly positive
variables, such as raw intensities or contents, can be transformed with
either transformation.
}
\section{Robust fit}{

The parameter \eqn{\lambda} of each variable is fitted as follows:
\enumerate{
\item The variable is pre-standardized (see Details).
\item An initial \eqn{\lambda} (between -4 and 6) minimizes a robust
distance (Tukey's biweight) between the sorted, robustly standardized
transformed values and the quantiles of the normal distribution. For
this step, the transformation is continued linearly on the side that
it compresses (the upper side for \eqn{\lambda < 1}, the lower side
for \eqn{\lambda > 1}), beyond the point where the transformed value
is 1.5 times that of the quartile, so that outliers on that side
cannot dictate \eqn{\lambda}.
\item The transformed values are standardized with Huber's M-estimates of
location and scale, and those farther from the center than the
\code{quantile} of the absolute value of a standard normal variable (2.58
for the default 0.99) are given weight zero (first with the rectified
transformation of step 2). \eqn{\lambda} is then re-estimated by
maximum likelihood on the others. This is repeated \code{nbsteps} times.
\item With \code{standardize = TRUE}, the transformed variable is standardized by
the mean and standard deviation of these inliers.
}

With \code{robust = FALSE}, \eqn{\lambda} is the classical maximum likelihood
estimate (Box and Cox, 1964; Yeo and Johnson, 2000): it maximizes the
normal likelihood of all the transformed values, and the transformed
variable is standardized by the mean and standard deviation of all the
values (\code{quantile} and \code{nbsteps} are not used). For the Box-Cox
transformation, this is the \eqn{\lambda} of \code{\link[MASS:boxcox]{MASS::boxcox()}}. Outliers
then pull \eqn{\lambda} toward the value that brings them closer to the
other values: the transformed regular values are skewed, and the outliers
can be hidden among them (see the examples).

Variables with fewer than 5 values or no spread (zero MAD) are left
unchanged. Missing values are ignored by the fit, and stay missing.
}

\examples{
# 300 right-skewed values on both sides of 0 (a gamma distribution shifted
# by -2) and 10 low outliers near -6
set.seed(6)
x <- c(rgamma(300, shape = 2) - 2, rnorm(10, mean = -6, sd = 0.3))
group <- rep(c("regular", "outlier"), c(300, 10))
ggplot2::ggplot(data.frame(x, group), ggplot2::aes(x)) +
  ggplot2::geom_density(fill = "#2166ac", alpha = 0.3) +
  ggplot2::geom_rug(ggplot2::aes(colour = group)) +
  ggplot2::scale_colour_manual(values = c(outlier = "#b2182b", regular = "#2166ac"))

# the low outliers balance the long right tail: the classical fit finds
# lambda close to 1, no transformation; the robust fit compresses the
# right tail (lambda below 1)
classical <- robust_yeojohnson(x, robust = FALSE)
robust <- robust_yeojohnson(x)
round(c(classical = classical$summary$lambda, robust = robust$summary$lambda), 2)

# the classical transformation leaves the regular values skewed to the
# right and the outliers near 3 standard deviations; the robust one makes
# the regular values symmetric and leaves the outliers far out
transformed <- data.frame(
  fit = rep(c("classical", "robust"), each = length(x)),
  value = c(classical$transformation[[1]], robust$transformation[[1]]),
  group = rep(group, 2)
)
ggplot2::ggplot(transformed, ggplot2::aes(value)) +
  ggplot2::geom_density(fill = "#2166ac", alpha = 0.3) +
  ggplot2::geom_rug(ggplot2::aes(colour = group)) +
  ggplot2::scale_colour_manual(values = c(outlier = "#b2182b", regular = "#2166ac")) +
  ggplot2::facet_wrap(~fit, ncol = 1)
regular <- transformed$group == "regular"
# the skewness of the transformed regular values, and the outlier closest
# to them
round(tapply(transformed$value[regular], transformed$fit[regular], medcouple), 2)
round(tapply(transformed$value[!regular], transformed$fit[!regular], max), 2)

data(forageLIBS)
spectra <- snv(forageLIBS[-(1:14)])$correction
wl <- as.numeric(names(spectra))
# the Mg II lines at 279.55 and 280.27 nm: after SNV, most channels have
# negative values in some spectra, which Box-Cox cannot transform
mg <- spectra[which(wl > 279 & wl < 281)]
sum(vapply(mg, min, numeric(1)) < 0)
fit <- robust_yeojohnson(mg[1:300, ])
summary(fit$summary$lambda)
# the skewness of the channels, before and after
summary(sapply(mg[1:300, ], medcouple))
summary(sapply(fit$transformation, medcouple))
# new spectra, with the transformation of the first 300
dim(predict(fit, mg[301:368, ]))
}
